## 1 Introduction

Binary neutron star mergers can feature some of the most extreme magnetic field configurations in the universe (see, e.g., Baiotti and Rezzolla, 2017 for a review). Although magnetic field strengths in the inspiralling systems may be small (and dynamically unimportant), turbulent small-scale process can potentially amplify the magnetic field during merger (Price and Rosswog, 2006; Kiuchi et al., 2015), reaching dynamically important field strengths (Kiuchi et al., 2018; Aguilera-Miret et al., 2022). Despite their importance, the magnetic field topology after merger remains largely unconstrained.

While several numerical relativity simulations of magnetized binary neutron star mergers have been performed (e.g., Kiuchi et al., 2014; Dionysopoulou et al., 2015; Palenzuela et al., 2015; Ciolfi et al., 2017; Most et al., 2019; Palenzuela et al., 2022; Werneck et al., 2022; Combi and Siegel, 2023; Kiuchi et al., 2022), turbulent magnetic field amplification during the collision (Price and Rosswog, 2006; Kiuchi et al., 2015; Aguilera-Miret et al., 2020), which likely dominates the post-merger magnetic field geometry (Aguilera-Miret et al., 2022), is not fully captured by current simulations (Kiuchi et al., 2018). Although magnetic fields in the inspiral are not likely to strongly affect the orbital dynamics or gravitational wave emission (Ioka and Taniguchi, 2000; Zhu et al., 2020), they could produce electromagnetic precursors prior to the merger (see, e.g., Hansen and Lyutikov, 2001; Tsang et al., 2012; Palenzuela et al., 2013; Carrasco and Shibata, 2020; Most and Philippov, 2020; Zhang, 2020; Most and Philippov, 2022). In addition, magnetic fields undoubtedly play a major role in the dynamics of the post-merger evolution and its connection to electromagnetic afterglows and the production of gamma-ray bursts (GRBs) (see respectively, e.g., Metzger, 2020 and Berger, 2014 for recent reviews). In particular, magnetic fields might drive winds from the (meta-)stable remnant star formed in some mergers (Metzger et al., 2018; Ciolfi and Kalinani, 2020) and facilitate most of the mass ejection from post-merger accretion disks (e.g., Fernandez and Metzger, 2013; Martin et al., 2015; Siegel and Metzger,2017; Fernandez et al., 2019). In regimes in which a remnant neutron star is rotationally supported against gravitational collapse, magnetic fields provide various means of transporting angular momentum, critically affecting the lifetime of the system (Gill et al., 2019; Margalit et al., 2022). They also enable jet launching ultimately resulting in (short) GRBs (Rezzolla et al., 2011; Ruiz et al., 2016).

Over the past years, the connection between binary neutron star mergers and GRBs has been confirmed directly by the coincident detection of the short GRB GRB170817A with the gravitational-wave (GW) event GW170817 (Abbott et al., 2017). Adding to this picture, Chirenti et al. (2023) recently reported the discovery of kilohertz quasi-periodic oscillations (QPOs) in a small subset of previously detected sGRBs. Assuming a neutron star merger as the origin of these sGRBs, one possible place to look for kilohertz QPOs are the oscillations of a (meta-)stable hypermassive neutron star (HMNS) formed during the collision (Chireni et al., 2019). These have been shown to be quasi-universally related to properties of the inspiralling neutron stars and the dense matter they contain (e.g., Bauswein et al. (2012); Bauswein and Janka (2012); Takami et al. (2014); Bernuzzi et al. (2015); Raithel and Most (2022)). If this association could be made, it would potentially open up a new way of placing constraints on the dense matter equation of state (e.g., Ozel and Freire, 2016), provided the redshift of these events could be determined (Chireni et al., 2023).

Despite these exciting prospects, there are a number of questions about the connection between the relativistic outflow that powers the gamma-ray emission and the post-merger oscillations. Post-merger oscillations are most pronounced in the first few milliseconds after the collisions (see, e.g., Nedora et al., 2021; Most et al., 2021 for recent discussions). However, launching a relativistic outflow or jet from a stellar merger typically happens on longer timescales (Siegel et al., 2014; Ciolfi, 2020), when these oscillations have long decayed away. Therefore, launching a relativistic jet-like outflow, that could contain imprints of the oscillating HMNS at its base would require establishing a strongly collimated magnetic funnel region immediately after merger (Shibata et al., 2011; Mosta et al., 2020). Due to the difficulty capturing small-scale dynamo physics in present simulations, such a scenario can currently only be realized in numerical simulations with subgrid dynamo prescriptions (e.g., Tomei et al., 2020; Shibata et al., 2021).

We here present binary neutron star merger simulations, which are augmented with a sub-grid dynamo prescription enabling us to freely adjust the magnetic field strengths produced during the merger (see Sec. 2). Using this idealized approach, we provide a proof-of-principle demonstration that for extreme magnetic field strengths in excess of B\\simeq 10^{17}\\,\\mathrm{G}, HMNS can quasi-periodically emit flares (see Sec. 3.1) and relativistic outflows (see Sec. 3.2), which feature kilohertz sub-structures (see Sec. 3.3). Despite the caveats of using such an effective approach, our work clearly demonstrates the importance of magnetic field amplification and the resulting post-merger topology in understanding sGRB production, particularly in the earliest post-merger phases.

## 2 Methods

We perform numerical simulations of neutron star coalescence in full general relativity using the Frankfurt/IllinoisGRMHD (FIL) code (Most et al., 2019; Etienne et al., 2015). FIL solves the equations of general-relativistic magnetohydrodynamics (GRMHD) in dynamical spacetimes (Duez et al., 2005), evolved using the Z4c formulation (Hilditch et al., 2013). Furthermore, the code makes use of high-order numerical methods allowing us to capture especially magnetic field dynamics accurately at lower numerical resolutions (Del Zanna et al., 2007). Following the GRMHD benchmark runs presented in (Most et al., 2019), we perform simulations on a mesh-refined grid with the highest numerical resolution of \\Delta x=250\\,\\mathrm{m}. We further make use of a neutrino leakage scheme (Ruffert et al., 1996), although neutrino aspects are not important for this work. We adopt the DD2 equation of state having a radius of R_{1.4}=13.2\\,\\mathrm{km}(Hempel and Schaffner-Bielich, 2010), as well as the APR{}_{\\mathrm{LDP}} equation of state with R_{1.4}=12.2\\,\\mathrm{km} for comparison (Schneider et al., 2019). We further consider a GW170817-like configuration with a chirp mass \\mathcal{M}=1.186\\,M_{\\odot} and a mass ratio q=0.85 at an initial separation of 45\\,\\mathrm{km}. The initial data is computed using LORENE1 (see also, e.g., Papenfort et al., 2021). Furthermore, we start with an initial poloidal magnetic field in the pre-merger stars given by the toroidal vector potential A_{\\phi}=A_{0}\\max(0,\\left(p-10^{-3}p_{\\mathrm{max}}\\right)^{2}), where p is the pressure and p_{\\mathrm{max}} its maximum value inside the inspiraling stars (Liu et al., 2008). The constant A_{0} is chosen such as to obtain field strengths of around 10^{15}\\,\\mathrm{G}. More details on the setup can be found in Most et al. (2019) and Most and Raithel (2021).

